---
import LearningLayout from '../../../layouts/LearningLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../../components/Icon.astro';
import { contextFindings, equityRows } from '../../../lib/food-evidence';
---
<LearningLayout title="Context, mechanisms and missing voices" section="context" intro="The example trial outcome is purchasing. These separate, fictional qualitative materials illustrate how a synthesis could examine the circumstances behind it.">
  <div class="learning-notice"><Icon name="info"/><div><strong>Reading the qualitative examples</strong><p>All qualitative materials below are invented. They have not been gathered, coded or assessed with CERQual. They show how different kinds of findings could be presented together.</p></div></div>
  <div class="context-grid">{contextFindings.map(finding=><article class="context-card"><span class="status-pill">Fictional qualitative finding</span><h2>{finding.title}</h2><p>{finding.finding}</p><h3>Relationship to the question</h3><p>{finding.relationship}</p><dl><div><dt>Illustrative source</dt><dd>{finding.source}</dd></div><div><dt>Limitation</dt><dd>{finding.limitation}</dd></div></dl></article>)}</div>
  <section class="learning-section"><p class="eyebrow">Equity and completeness</p><h2>Which information is missing?</h2><p class="learning-section-lead">These are fields a review could extract. They are not observed subgroup effects. “Not reported” does not mean that a group was absent or unaffected.</p><div class="data-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Equity extraction fields table"><table class="learning-table"><caption>Illustrative equity extraction fields and the limits of this worked example</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Dimension</th><th scope="col">What the field would capture</th><th scope="col">What is unavailable here</th></tr></thead><tbody>{equityRows.map(row=><tr><th scope="row">{row.dimension}</th><td>{row.reported}</td><td>{row.missing}</td></tr>)}<tr><th scope="row">Racism and structural exclusion</th><td>Who shaped the intervention; exclusionary rules or conditions; whose knowledge is represented.</td><td>No community data or subgroup estimates exist in these fixtures. The simulator does not assign behaviour or effects by race or ethnicity.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>
  <section class="learning-two-col learning-section"><div><h2>Local applicability needs its own assessment.</h2><p>The six fictional trials concern household-specific offers. Changing where shops are located or when they open is a different intervention. The trial risk ratio does not estimate these changes.</p><p>A review team would check the population, setting, offer, comparator and outcome before applying findings to a local decision.</p></div><div class="boundary-card"><h3>Connection to the scenario</h3><p>Affordability, travel time and opening hours are explicit scenario assumptions. The model does not quantify cultural acceptability or trust.</p><a class="text-link" href="/learning/food-access/scenario/">Inspect the scenario assumptions <Icon/></a></div></section>
</LearningLayout>
